xmodel/ideation-20260902T1608Z-sol56.md:9:The right statement is: **every boundary instrument currently built, including Moh's published skeleton, is one-directional.** `N-CEILING` gives `N <= U`; extra structure below `D_1` adds contact and lowers `N`; the filter `N <= 16` therefore cannot reject a skeleton (`xmodel/integration16-coordinator-fable51-20260902.md:38-54`; `xmodel/n-on-the-tree-review-grok46-20260902.md:143-153`). The 418 rows are necessary H2 realization targets, not maps, and “pinned to 4” consumes the H2 floor `N >= 4` (`xmodel/n-on-the-tree-review-grok46-20260902.md:191-218,334-345`). No ceiling has been proved.
xmodel/ideation-20260902T1608Z-sol56.md:21:My allocation answer is consequently direct. Give `D_1` one bounded, exact pilot. If it does not expose a positive Jacobian deficiency at the first post-`delta_1` levels, stop treating a universal `D <= C(N)` as the primary proof target. Move the proof flagship to the Moh endgame—uniform elimination of finite-depth, unbounded-size skeletons—and run the exact same `D_1` machinery backwards on the counterexample side. Keep a small global-invariant probe, but do not fund five speculative ceiling languages at once. The depth count is finite; the coefficients and sizes are not. That is an elimination/realization problem, not evidence that another scalar ledger is waiting to be found.
xmodel/ideation-20260902T1608Z-sol56.md:91:This also connects two lanes that the present ranking keeps separate. For a fixed candidate `g`, finding `f` is the linear Hamiltonian equation `[f,g]=1`; the banked kappa class vanishes exactly when a polynomial mate exists (`xmodel/hamiltonian-kappa-gate-20260824.md:49-80`). The kappa module has no universal receiver and remains a bad global ceiling strategy (`xmodel/hamiltonian-kappa-gate-20260824.md:180-198`), but on one typed `D_1` skeleton it is an exact obstruction/certificate engine. Candidate (d) should therefore be lowered as a global flow proposal and reused as the local solver inside candidate (a).
