xmodel/as109-ainfinity-review-grok-20260824.md:187:Finiteness and exactness of the support test. A named finite support has finitely many monomials, hence finitely many pairwise tying hyperplanes in `R^2`, hence finitely many candidate 0-cells, with rational coordinates determined by integer coefficient valuations. Axes add two univariate Newton polygons, also finite. For each retained negative weight, emptiness of the saturated initial system is a finite Gröbner computation over a finitely generated coefficient ring (after including `det J=1` if desired). Exact for the vanishing direction. If some initial system survives, its `in_w(I)`-length plus lifting is the exact residual contribution, not an existence proof of a global lift. The producer states both.
